1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,156
Your universe file is four hundred lines long.

2
00:00:04,056 --> 00:00:06,985
It works, and it's getting hard to find anything in.

3
00:00:08,785 --> 00:00:09,944
Today we tidy up.

4
00:00:10,844 --> 00:00:12,678
The jobs get a file of their own,

5
00:00:13,128 --> 00:00:15,146
and so does each side's dialogue.

6
00:00:16,946 --> 00:00:19,000
And then there's a reward for the housekeeping.

7
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:23,832
We print the whole world, as a book, a script, and a website.

8
00:00:25,032 --> 00:00:27,544
Here's the whole idea, in four lines.

9
00:00:28,444 --> 00:00:30,501
A chapter heading, a fence,

10
00:00:30,951 --> 00:00:33,940
and inside it, File, and a file's name.

11
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:38,317
When the game starts, it reads that file,

12
00:00:38,767 --> 00:00:41,619
and puts its records into this chapter.

13
00:00:42,519 --> 00:00:44,507
As far as the game's concerned,

14
00:00:44,957 --> 00:00:46,255
you typed them right here.

15
00:00:48,055 --> 00:00:51,920
There are three rules, and I'll show you each one as we go.

16
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:55,959
A new file, jobs dot a m d.

17
00:00:56,859 --> 00:01:00,402
I cut the six jobs out of the main file, and paste them in.

18
00:01:02,202 --> 00:01:03,317
Now for rule one.

19
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:07,191
In the main file, a job has three hashes,

20
00:01:07,641 --> 00:01:10,332
because it sits under a title and a chapter.

21
00:01:11,232 --> 00:01:14,447
In a chapter file there's no title and no chapter.

22
00:01:15,347 --> 00:01:18,695
So every record here starts with one hash.

23
00:01:20,495 --> 00:01:22,222
Replace All does it,

24
00:01:22,672 --> 00:01:26,393
and it tells me six changes, which is how many jobs I have.

25
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:31,149
Back in the main file, the chapter gets its fence.

26
00:01:32,349 --> 00:01:35,104
Dialogue is the big one, with twenty records.

27
00:01:36,004 --> 00:01:38,122
I'm sorting them by who's speaking,

28
00:01:38,572 --> 00:01:40,923
one file for each side, in a folder.

29
00:01:42,723 --> 00:01:44,766
A captain's lines go with her side.

30
00:01:45,666 --> 00:01:47,367
That's a habit, not a rule.

31
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:51,130
And here's rule two.

32
00:01:52,030 --> 00:01:54,672
Three files, one line, with commas.

33
00:01:56,472 --> 00:01:59,779
You'll also see a line for each file, one under another.

34
00:02:00,679 --> 00:02:01,776
That works as well,

35
00:02:02,226 --> 00:02:04,355
and the game reads every one of them.

36
00:02:05,555 --> 00:02:06,504
Now we run lint.

37
00:02:07,404 --> 00:02:08,771
It names every file,

38
00:02:09,221 --> 00:02:11,119
and every one of them is clean.

39
00:02:12,919 --> 00:02:14,882
Remember the signal from Lecture Seven.

40
00:02:15,782 --> 00:02:19,212
An answer sends it, and a story beat waits for it.

41
00:02:20,112 --> 00:02:21,974
Those two are in different files now.

42
00:02:23,774 --> 00:02:27,184
Lint reads the mission as one thing, the way the game does,

43
00:02:27,634 --> 00:02:29,547
so it sees the two ends meet.

44
00:02:30,447 --> 00:02:33,412
Misspell the word at either end, and it tells you.

45
00:02:35,212 --> 00:02:36,548
And then there's rule three.

46
00:02:37,448 --> 00:02:40,188
A chapter reads from files, or it holds records.

47
00:02:41,088 --> 00:02:42,705
Both together do work,

48
00:02:43,155 --> 00:02:45,466
but then one chapter lives in two places.

49
00:02:46,366 --> 00:02:47,439
So keep to one.

50
00:02:48,639 --> 00:02:49,910
One new thing for the crew.

51
00:02:50,810 --> 00:02:52,928
A captain is hailed at a station.

52
00:02:53,378 --> 00:02:55,295
A member of the cast doesn't need one.

53
00:02:57,095 --> 00:02:58,946
This is the relay's duty voice.

54
00:02:59,846 --> 00:03:01,746
It has a face, a color,

55
00:03:02,196 --> 00:03:05,184
and a scene, which is the record that speaks for it.

56
00:03:06,984 --> 00:03:09,796
The scene goes in a dialogue file, like any other.

57
00:03:10,696 --> 00:03:12,399
Notice there are no guards.

58
00:03:13,299 --> 00:03:14,860
The cast keeps no standing,

59
00:03:15,310 --> 00:03:19,271
so use them for what a crew should always be able to find out.

60
00:03:20,471 --> 00:03:25,189
And one more file, called exactly lore dot a m d.

61
00:03:26,989 --> 00:03:28,435
There are no fences in this one.

62
00:03:29,335 --> 00:03:31,041
A heading is a page,

63
00:03:31,491 --> 00:03:33,846
and the words under it are what the crew reads.

64
00:03:35,646 --> 00:03:37,838
The crew's handheld has a Library,

65
00:03:38,288 --> 00:03:40,834
and this file becomes a chapter in it.

66
00:03:42,634 --> 00:03:44,579
Each page is drawn like a briefing,

67
00:03:45,029 --> 00:03:48,053
with paragraphs, and a table where it helps.

68
00:03:48,953 --> 00:03:51,078
This heading has no square brackets,

69
00:03:51,528 --> 00:03:54,911
so it's a heading inside the page, not a page of its own.

70
00:03:56,711 --> 00:03:59,370
Put in what somebody who lives here would know.

71
00:04:00,270 --> 00:04:02,495
Keep the secrets of your story out.

72
00:04:03,695 --> 00:04:04,712
Now for the reward.

73
00:04:05,612 --> 00:04:09,359
s b s docs, the folder, a title,

74
00:04:09,809 --> 00:04:11,556
and I ask for all four editions.

75
00:04:13,356 --> 00:04:15,655
The prose one is the book.

76
00:04:16,555 --> 00:04:18,950
The catalog sorts everything by kind.

77
00:04:19,850 --> 00:04:22,772
The screenplay is only the conversations.

78
00:04:23,222 --> 00:04:25,886
Read one aloud. It's the best test there is.

79
00:04:27,686 --> 00:04:29,180
And the bible is the plot.

80
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:31,365
Look at this beat.

81
00:04:31,815 --> 00:04:36,401
It knows the call leads here, across the two files.

82
00:04:38,201 --> 00:04:39,376
Then there's the website.

83
00:04:40,276 --> 00:04:42,930
One page for each file, a search box,

84
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:46,003
and every answer is a link to where it goes.

85
00:04:46,903 --> 00:04:50,062
Zip the folder and send it to whoever's writing with you.

86
00:04:51,262 --> 00:04:53,423
And the game is the same game.

87
00:04:54,323 --> 00:04:57,265
There's the same station, with the same buttons.

88
00:04:58,165 --> 00:05:01,836
The levy comes from one file now, and the jobs from another.

89
00:05:03,636 --> 00:05:05,681
Here's the Library, with my pages in it.

90
00:05:07,481 --> 00:05:09,750
So, a last word about lint.

91
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:14,162
After a split, it misses a couple of things it used to catch,

92
00:05:14,612 --> 00:05:16,311
like a file name that isn't there.

93
00:05:17,211 --> 00:05:20,451
The list on the page is longer today for that reason.

94
00:05:21,651 --> 00:05:22,654
Now it's your turn.

95
00:05:23,554 --> 00:05:27,482
Split your universe, and run lint until every file says clean.

96
00:05:28,382 --> 00:05:30,931
Write a voice anyone can ask for directions,

97
00:05:31,381 --> 00:05:33,005
and four pages of lore.

98
00:05:34,805 --> 00:05:39,057
Then print the screenplay, and read one conversation out loud.

99
00:05:39,957 --> 00:05:42,526
Change a line because of what you heard.

100
00:05:44,326 --> 00:05:46,639
Next time, the crew gets off the ship.
